    <script type="application/ld+json">{"mainEntity":{"headline":"Stromal cell senescence augments haematopoietic cell fitness in clonal haematopoiesis","description":"Microenvironment remodelling impacts tumour growth and metastasis, but whether remodelling promotes pre-malignant clonal fitness remains unknown. Here, using single-cell RNA-sequencing of the bone-marrow microenvironment in a mouse model of DNMT3A-mutant clonal haematopoiesis (CH), we identify mesenchymal stromal cells (MSCs) in a molecular state of cellular senescence. Elevated bone-marrow MSC senescence is also observed in humans with CH driven by several common somatic mutations. MSC senescence is induced by mutant haematopoietic cells in a contact-independent manner through production of soluble factors including TNF-α and IL-6. These cytokines activate a Stat3-driven pathway that is necessary and sufficient for MSC senescence induction. Genetic or pharmacological depletion of senescent non-haematopoietic cells reduces the burden of CH and delays progression to myeloid neoplasia. Our findings show that microenvironment remodelling modifies pre-malignant clonal fitness and identifies disruption of the crosstalk between pre-malignant cells and their niche as a cancer prevention strategy. Mistry et al. propose that Dnmt3a-mutant haematopoietic cells induce mesenchymal stromal cell senescence. Removal of senescent non-haematopoietic cells reduces Dnmt3a-mutant haematopoiesis.","datePublished":"2026-08-06T00:00:00Z","dateModified":"2026-08-06T00:00:00Z","pageStart":"1656","pageEnd":"1670","license":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/","sameAs":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41556-026-02025-4","keywords":["Senescence","Haematopoietic stem cells","Mesenchymal stem cells","Life Sciences","general","Cell Biology","Cancer Research","Developmental Biology","Stem Cells"],"image":["https://media.springernature.com/lw1200/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41556-026-02025-4/MediaObjects/41556_2026